              "locator": "Section 3.3, meridian and oriented normal-disk convention, pp. 26-30"
            },
            {
              "title": "Alessia Cattabriga and Stefano Francaviglia, Knot Theory and Applications: Lecture Notes",
              "url": "https://poisson.phc.dm.unipi.it/~camponovo/libri/knot.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 4.4, peripheral orientation convention"
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "Direct clutching argument: cut S^1 into an interval, trivialize the oriented rank-two bundle there, and remove the endpoint gluing using path-connectedness of GL+(2,R). The proof is choice-free."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-knot-meridian-and-preferred-longitude",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Meridian and preferred longitude",
        "deps": [
          "def-knot-exterior",
          "def-oriented-link-in-s-three-and-ambient-isotopy",
          "def-normal-and-conormal-bundles-of-an-embedded-submanifold"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "justified_by": [],
        "statement": "For a smooth oriented knot with chosen exterior E_K and boundary torus T_K, a meridian mu is the oriented boundary of a normal disk, with the normal-plane orientation determined by the orientations of the knot and the three-sphere. A preferred longitude, when specified by a curve, is a simple closed curve on T_K whose projection to the oriented core has degree +1 and whose class maps to zero in H_1(E_K; Z). This defines the zero-homology longitude condition without asserting existence; the homology theorem on this page proves existence and uniqueness. Peripheral fundamental-group subgroups are recorded only up to conjugacy under changes of base path.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Alessia Cattabriga and Stefano Francaviglia, Knot Theory and Applications: Lecture Notes",
              "url": "https://poisson.phc.dm.unipi.it/~camponovo/libri/knot.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 4.4, meridian, longitude, and peripheral-system conventions"
            },
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Introduction to Knot Theory",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/notes/knot_theory.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 3.3, definition of an oriented meridian, pp. 29-30"
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "Define the meridian from the oriented normal disk and the longitude as a degree-one boundary-parallel zero-homology condition. Leave existence and uniqueness to the later theorem, avoiding a circular definition."
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-knot-tubular-exterior-and-boundary",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Tubular exterior and boundary torus",
        "deps": [
          "def-knot-exterior",
          "lem-knot-normal-circle-bundle-trivialization",
          "def-tubular-neighbourhood-of-an-embedded-submanifold",
          "thm-tubular-neighbourhood-theorem-in-a-smooth-ambient-manifold",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "justified_by": [],
        "statement": "Assume countable choice. For every smooth oriented knot K in S^3, there are an oriented normal-bundle trivialization, a tubular map Phi defined on an open neighbourhood containing S^1 x D^2_{2 epsilon} for some epsilon>0, and a closed tubular neighbourhood N(K)=Phi(S^1 x D^2_epsilon). This N(K) is diffeomorphic to S^1 x D^2, its boundary T_K is diffeomorphic to S^1 x S^1, and its exterior E_K=S^3 minus int N(K) is a compact smooth oriented three-manifold with boundary T_K. The open complement S^3 minus K strongly deformation retracts onto E_K.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Alessia Cattabriga and Stefano Francaviglia, Knot Theory and Applications: Lecture Notes",
              "url": "https://poisson.phc.dm.unipi.it/~camponovo/libri/knot.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 4.1, compact exterior and boundary-torus convention"
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "Use the published smooth tubular-neighbourhood theorem under its exact AC_omega hypothesis, the local oriented normal trivialization, compactness of S^1 to choose a uniform disk radius, and radial retraction in the punctured disk fibres."
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-knot-exterior-has-peripheral-torus",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "The knot exterior has a peripheral torus basis",
        "deps": [
          "def-knot-exterior",
          "def-knot-meridian-and-preferred-longitude",
          "lem-knot-tubular-exterior-and-boundary",
          "thm-cellular-homology-computes-singular-homology",
          "def-cellular-homology",
          "def-cellular-boundary-from-three-consecutive-skeleta",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "thm-cellular-boundary-is-the-incidence-degree-matrix"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "justified_by": [],
        "statement": "Assume countable choice. For a smooth oriented knot with a chosen product tubular neighbourhood N(K) = S^1 x D^2, let mu be the positive fibre meridian and let sigma be the product section s -> (s,1) of T_K = partial N(K) = partial E_K. Then T_K is a torus and [mu],[sigma] form a basis of H_1(T_K; Z). The curve sigma is a product section; this statement does not assert that it is the preferred zero-homology longitude.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Alessia Cattabriga and Stefano Francaviglia, Knot Theory and Applications: Lecture Notes",
              "url": "https://poisson.phc.dm.unipi.it/~camponovo/libri/knot.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 4.4, peripheral homology basis discussion"
            },
            {
              "title": "Allen Hatcher, Algebraic Topology",
              "url": "https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATch2.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 2.2, Example 2.36, cellular homology of closed orientable surfaces"
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "Identify the boundary with S^1 x S^1 and compute its first homology from its one-vertex, two-edge, one-face CW structure; the commutator attaching map has zero cellular boundary."
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-knot-exterior-homology-and-longitude",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Homology and preferred longitude of a knot exterior",
        "deps": [
          "def-knot-exterior",
          "def-knot-meridian-and-preferred-longitude",
          "lem-knot-tubular-exterior-and-boundary",
          "lem-knot-exterior-has-peripheral-torus",
          "thm-alexander-duality-for-compact-locally-contractible-subsets-of-a-sphere",
          "thm-universal-coefficient-theorem-for-cohomology-over-a-pid",
          "thm-mayer-vietoris-sequence-in-singular-homology",
          "cor-homology-of-spheres",
          "cor-homotopic-maps-induce-the-same-map-on-singular-homology",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-oriented-link-in-s-three-and-ambient-isotopy",
          "prop-the-image-of-a-smooth-embedding-is-an-embedded-submanifold",
          "def-embedded-submanifold-and-slice-chart",
          "prop-singular-chains-and-homology-are-covariantly-functorial"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "justified_by": [],
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let K:S^1 -> S^3 be a smooth oriented embedding and write its quotient normal bundle as nu_K=K^*TS^3/dK(TS^1). Choose an orientation-preserving trivialization nu_K=S^1 x R^2 and a tubular map Phi:Omega -> S^3, meaning a smooth embedding from an open neighbourhood Omega in nu_K of the zero section onto an open neighbourhood of K(S^1), with Phi(s,0)=K(s), such that Omega contains S^1 x D^2_{2 epsilon} for some epsilon>0; here D^2_r={z in R^2: ||z||<=r}. Set N(K)=Phi(S^1 x D^2_epsilon), E_K=S^3 minus int N(K), T_K=partial E_K, i:T_K -> E_K the inclusion, p:T_K -> S^1 the product projection, and mu the positive boundary of a normal disk oriented by K and S^3. A homology class is primitive if it is not m times another class for any integer |m|>1. Then H_1(E_K; Z) is infinite cyclic generated by [mu]. The kernel of i_*:H_1(T_K; Z) -> H_1(E_K; Z) is a primitive rank-one direct summand generated by a unique class [lambda] with p_*[lambda]=1. It has a simple closed representative, is the preferred longitude, and ([mu],[lambda]) is a basis of H_1(T_K; Z).",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Allen Hatcher, Algebraic Topology",
              "url": "https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATch3.pdf",
              "locator": "Theorem 3.44 and Corollary 3.45, pp. 254-256"
            },
            {
              "title": "Alessia Cattabriga and Stefano Francaviglia, Knot Theory and Applications: Lecture Notes",
              "url": "https://poisson.phc.dm.unipi.it/~camponovo/libri/knot.pdf",
              "locator": "Sections 4.1 and 4.4, compact exterior and peripheral systems"
            },
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Introduction to Knot Theory",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/notes/knot_theory.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 3.3, linking and meridian conventions, pp. 26-30"
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "Apply Alexander duality to the smooth circle and the punctured complement, retract to the compact exterior, then use Mayer-Vietoris for the open cover int N(K) and S^3 minus K to identify the meridian generator and the unique primitive kernel longitude."
      }
