        "dependency_level": 4,
        "justified_by": [],
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Give the unit sphere S^3 = {(z_1,z_2) in C^2: |z_1|^2+|z_2|^2=1} its standard boundary orientation, and orient the standard unknot U(t)=(e^(2 pi i t),0) by increasing t in R/Z. For r>0, write D^2_r={w in C: |w|<=r}. Choose the closed tubular neighbourhood N(U)={(z_1,z_2) in S^3: |z_2|<=1/sqrt(2)}. Its exterior E_U={(z_1,z_2) in S^3: |z_2|>=1/sqrt(2)} is a solid torus diffeomorphic to D^2_{1/sqrt(2)} x S^1. On its boundary, the positive meridian is the phase loop in z_2, while the preferred longitude is the phase loop in z_1 at fixed z_2 phase; consequently H_1(E_U; Z) is infinite cyclic generated by [mu] and [lambda]=0.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Alessia Cattabriga and Stefano Francaviglia, Knot Theory and Applications: Lecture Notes",
              "url": "https://poisson.phc.dm.unipi.it/~camponovo/libri/knot.pdf",
              "locator": "Example 4.1.2 and Section 4.4"
            },
            {
              "title": "Allen Hatcher, Algebraic Topology",
              "url": "https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATch3.pdf",
              "locator": "Theorem 3.44 and Corollary 3.45, pp. 254-256"
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "Compute an explicit standard splitting of the unit three-sphere into two solid tori, then identify the meridian and preferred longitude in those product coordinates."
      },
      {
        "id": "cex-knot-kt-02-boundary-case",
        "kind": "counterexample",
        "title": "An arbitrary parallel push-off need not be preferred",
        "deps": [
          "def-knot-exterior",
          "def-knot-meridian-and-preferred-longitude",
